[2K[0m]8;; [38;5;222m[1m### [22m[39m[38;5;222m[1mBreaking Changes[22m[39m                                                                                                   [0m]8;;
[2K[0m]8;;                                                                                                                        [0m]8;;
[2K[0m]8;; [38;5;222m[1m### [22m[39m[38;5;222m[1mAdded[22m[39m                                                                                                              [0m]8;;
[2K[0m]8;;                                                                                                                        [0m]8;;
[2K[0m]8;; [38;5;109m- [39mNew [38;5;109mgpt-6-astra[39m prompt preset, written from scratch against the GPT-6 Astra prompting guide: every [38;5;109mgpt-6-astra[39m model [0m]8;;
[2K[0m]8;;   id (bare, [38;5;109m-fast[39m, dated snapshots, provider-prefixed, Bedrock [38;5;109mopenai.gpt-6-astra[39m, display name "GPT-6 Astra") now     [0m]8;;
[2K[0m]8;;   gets a full-core system prompt with an initiative section (bias to action, approval as the last step on a concrete   [0m]8;;
[2K[0m]8;;   result), explicit instruction precedence for skills and project files, an asynchronous-work section mapping Astra's  [0m]8;;
[2K[0m]8;;   async-tool training onto background sessions, monitors, child tasks, and detached eval cells (end the turn to wait;  [0m]8;;
[2K[0m]8;;   no wait tool), calibrated test-first verification, and an engineer-prose writing style with the guide's slop-phrase  [0m]8;;
[2K[0m]8;;   ban. [38;5;109mpromptPreset: "gpt-6-astra"[39m forces it.                                                                          [0m]8;;
[2K[0m]8;;                                                                                                                        [0m]8;;
[2K[0m]8;; [38;5;222m[1m### [22m[39m[38;5;222m[1mChanged[22m[39m                                                                                                            [0m]8;;
[2K[0m]8;;                                                                                                                        [0m]8;;
[2K[0m]8;; [38;5;222m[1m### [22m[39m[38;5;222m[1mFixed[22m[39m                                                                                                              [0m]8;;
[2K[0m]8;;                                                                                                                        [0m]8;;
[2K[0m]8;; [38;5;109m- [39mAnthropic Messages requests that carry deferred ([38;5;109mdefer_loading[39m) tools no longer fail with [38;5;109minvalid_request_error:     [0m]8;;
[2K[0m]8;;   [38;5;109mtools.N.tool_search_tool_bm25_20251119.name: Input should be 'tool_search_tool_bm25'[39m. The injected native            [0m]8;;
[2K[0m]8;;   tool-search server tool is now named [38;5;109mtool_search_tool_bm25[39m as the API contract requires; the local [38;5;109mtool_search[39m       [0m]8;;
[2K[0m]8;;   custom tool is unchanged.                                                                                            [0m]8;;
[2K[0m]8;; [38;5;109m- [39mPrompt surfaces no longer ship the same guidance twice per turn: the [38;5;109mTask_Management[39m section stops re-sending the    [0m]8;;
[2K[0m]8;;   todo tool description, [38;5;109mupdate_goal[39m points at the goal audits instead of restating them, and the bash timeout policy  [0m]8;;
[2K[0m]8;;   hands the waiting doctrine to the terminal section. Roughly 1.5K tokens leave every turn with no rule removed.       [0m]8;;
[2K[0m]8;; [38;5;109m- [39mThe shared GPT eval-routing bridge no longer routes multi-call work to the [38;5;109mexec[39m/[38;5;109mwait[39m Code Mode tools that were       [0m]8;;
[2K[0m]8;;   removed in favor of detached [38;5;109meval[39m cells; every GPT preset now points at [38;5;109meval[39m only.                                   [0m]8;;
[2K[0m]8;; [38;5;109m- [39mResuming a session left on a [38;5;109m-fast[39m catalog variant (or any model with an [38;5;109mupstreamModelId[39m) now restores that exact    [0m]8;;
[2K[0m]8;;   selection instead of its upstream base model, so the priority tier and the fast indicator survive the resume;        [0m]8;;
[2K[0m]8;;   assistant messages no longer override an explicit same-provider model selection during session restore.              [0m]8;;
[2K[0m]8;; [38;5;109m- [39mTTSR now interrupts a single streamed assistant message that repeats the same paragraph three times (a               [0m]8;;
[2K[0m]8;;   within-message narration loop such as re-announcing the same "now writing the DAG cell" step for minutes without     [0m]8;;
[2K[0m]8;;   ever issuing the tool call): the collapse guard gains a paragraph-repeat mechanism, truncates the message from the   [0m]8;;
[2K[0m]8;;   first repeat, and injects the usual recovery nudge. Scalar runs, short periods, and line cycles were the only        [0m]8;;
[2K[0m]8;;   mechanisms before, and blank lines reset line-cycle tracking, so paragraph-level loops streamed unchecked until the  [0m]8;;
[2K[0m]8;;   user aborted. Tool-argument streams are not affected.                                                                [0m]8;;
[2K[0m]8;;                                                                                                                        [0m]8;;
