[2K[0m]8;; [38;5;109m- [39m[38;5;109mbash[39m, [38;5;109mpowershell[39m, [38;5;109mworkflow[39m and [38;5;109mmonitor[39m now run only inside eval cells whenever the [38;5;109meval[39m tool is available, with no   [0m]8;;
[2K[0m]8;;   setting to enable it. They leave the model's direct tool list and are called as [38;5;109mtool.bash(...)[39m, [38;5;109mtool.workflow(...)[39m   [0m]8;;
[2K[0m]8;;   and [38;5;109mtool.monitor(...)[39m; hooks and permission checks still apply, a direct call returns a hint naming the eval form,   [0m]8;;
[2K[0m]8;;   and a session without [38;5;109meval[39m (codemode disabled, or a child agent whose allowlist omits it) keeps all four directly    [0m]8;;
[2K[0m]8;;   callable. The prompt surfaces that document these tools render the reachable call form to match, and no session is   [0m]8;;
[2K[0m]8;;   told about a tool its registry does not hold.                                                                        [0m]8;;
[2K[0m]8;; [38;5;109m- [39mThe wait-as-subscription guidance moved from the model presets into the [38;5;109meval[39m tool description, where it can name the [0m]8;;
[2K[0m]8;;   reachable [38;5;109mtool.monitor(...)[39m form. Because the preset rule was gated on [38;5;109mmonitor[39m being directly selectable, it would   [0m]8;;
[2K[0m]8;;   otherwise have stopped rendering entirely once monitor became eval-only.                                             [0m]8;;
[2K[0m]8;; [38;5;109m- [39mPer-model system prompt presets for Claude Opus 4.5-4.8, Claude Opus 5, and GLM 5.2/5.3 are audited against their    [0m]8;;
[2K[0m]8;;   prompting guides and the dieted shared core: the Opus 5 core is rebuilt on the Fable 5.1 skeleton with the Opus 5    [0m]8;;
[2K[0m]8;;   guide behaviors stated once each and gains the guide's outcome-first final-summary shape; Opus 4.7/4.8 keep only     [0m]8;;
[2K[0m]8;;   their documented deltas and gain the same-turn subagent fan-out direction; Opus 4.6 drops tuning text the core now   [0m]8;;
[2K[0m]8;;   carries; GLM 5.2/5.3 share one builder, gain the eval/monitor execution-tooling stance, and lose the lineage         [0m]8;;
[2K[0m]8;;   preamble, undefined-mode reference, and unconditional todo procedure. The shared core gains a conditional delegation [0m]8;;
[2K[0m]8;;   rule and states the auto-compaction mechanism behind the context-limits rule.                                        [0m]8;;
[2K[0m]8;; [38;5;109m- [39mThe Kimi K3 prompt preset is rebuilt on the Fable 5.1 skeleton for Moonshot's documented K3 "excessive               [0m]8;;
[2K[0m]8;;   proactiveness": a Scope section makes the request the deliverable (pre-existing problems become follow-ups, tests    [0m]8;;
[2K[0m]8;;   are committed only where the task or repository calls for them), the ambiguity gate does the answer-independent work [0m]8;;
[2K[0m]8;;   first and then asks one question, a bounded failure cap stops improvisation after three failed approaches,           [0m]8;;
[2K[0m]8;;   delegation propagates a stop condition to subagents, and the K2.6-era act-bias repetition that outvoted those        [0m]8;;
[2K[0m]8;;   boundaries is gone - at 11 fewer Kimi K3 tokens than before.                                                         [0m]8;;
[2K[0m]8;; [38;5;222m[1m### [22m[39m[38;5;222m[1mFixed[22m[39m                                                                                                              [0m]8;;
[2K[0m]8;;                                                                                                                        [0m]8;;
[2K[0m]8;; [38;5;109m- [39mRPC logins now answer providers' mid-flow prompts over the extension UI dialog channel ([38;5;109mextension_ui_request[39m [38;5;109minput[39m   [0m]8;;
[2K[0m]8;;   for pasted codes, text, and secrets; [38;5;109mselect[39m for account choices) and release an unanswered dialog when the login     [0m]8;;
[2K[0m]8;;   settles, so Anthropic Claude Pro/Max and other prompt-driven OAuth flows complete through the browser callback       [0m]8;;
[2K[0m]8;;   instead of failing with [38;5;109mInteractive login input is not supported over RPC[39m and a dead callback port ([38;5;109m[4m#1316[24m[39m[38;5;241m            [0m]8;;
[2K[0m]8;;   [38;5;241m(https://github.com/code-yeongyu/senpi/issues/1316)[39m).                                                                [0m]8;;
[2K[0m]8;; [38;5;109m- [39mClaude SDK OAuth no longer loops on [38;5;109mNo conversation found with session ID[39m after a failed cold seed: a session id is  [0m]8;;
[2K[0m]8;;   resumed only once Claude Code acknowledged it (init or replay echo), and an id Claude Code reports missing is        [0m]8;;
[2K[0m]8;;   dropped instead of retried ([38;5;109m[4moh-my-openagent#7562[24m[39m[38;5;241m (https://github.com/code-yeongyu/oh-my-openagent/issues/7562)[39m).     [0m]8;;
[2K[0m]8;; [38;5;109m- [39mBundled Claude Code is now 2.1.259 via [38;5;109m@anthropic-ai/claude-agent-sdk[39m 0.3.259, so [38;5;109mclaude-sdk-oauth[39m sessions on       [0m]8;;
[2K[0m]8;;   [38;5;109mclaude-fable-5-1[39m no longer fail with the API 400 that required version 2.1.251 or newer ([38;5;109m[4m#1298[24m[39m[38;5;241m                       [0m]8;;
[2K[0m]8;;   [38;5;241m(https://github.com/code-yeongyu/senpi/issues/1298)[39m).                                                                [0m]8;;
[2K[0m]8;; [38;5;109m- [39mClaude SDK OAuth maps malformed or raw-string content entries to text (or an omission placeholder) instead of image  [0m]8;;
[2K[0m]8;;   blocks with undefined [38;5;109mmedia_type[39m/[38;5;109mdata[39m, which made Claude Code abort the next query ([38;5;109m[4moh-my-openagent#7660[24m[39m[38;5;241m             [0m]8;;
[2K[0m]8;;   [38;5;241m(https://github.com/code-yeongyu/oh-my-openagent/issues/7660)[39m).                                                      [0m]8;;
[2K[0m]8;; [38;5;109m- [39mA second [38;5;109mclaude-sdk-oauth[39m login now stores the newly issued OAuth tokens instead of a broken slot holding the        [0m]8;;
[2K[0m]8;;   managed placeholder, and no longer fails with [38;5;109mProvider is not configured: claude-sdk-oauth[39m when account rotation has [0m]8;;
[2K[0m]8;;   selected a single account ([38;5;109m[4m#1279[24m[39m[38;5;241m (https://github.com/code-yeongyu/senpi/issues/1279)[39m).                               [0m]8;;
[2K[0m]8;; [38;5;109m- [39mClaude SDK OAuth [38;5;109mis_error[39m results now trigger model fallback and multi-account failover instead of being treated as  [0m]8;;
[2K[0m]8;;   successful results ([38;5;109m[4m#1169[24m[39m[38;5;241m (https://github.com/code-yeongyu/senpi/issues/1169)[39m).                                      [0m]8;;
[2K[0m]8;; [38;5;109m- [39mClaude SDK OAuth now surfaces the SDK assistant's API error text and explains version-floor and unknown-model        [0m]8;;
[2K[0m]8;;   failures instead of reporting [38;5;109munknown[39m ([38;5;109m[4m#1298[24m[39m[38;5;241m (https://github.com/code-yeongyu/senpi/issues/1298)[39m,                    [0m]8;;
[2K[0m]8;;   [38;5;109m[4moh-my-openagent#7626[24m[39m[38;5;241m (https://github.com/code-yeongyu/oh-my-openagent/issues/7626)[39m).                                 [0m]8;;
[2K[0m]8;; [38;5;222m[1m### [22m[39m[38;5;222m[1mNew Features[22m[39m                                                                                                       [0m]8;;
[2K[0m]8;;                                                                                                                        [0m]8;;
[2K[0m]8;; [38;5;222m[1m### [22m[39m[38;5;222m[1mBreaking Changes[22m[39m                                                                                                   [0m]8;;
[2K[0m]8;;                                                                                                                        [0m]8;;
[2K[0m]8;; [38;5;222m[1m### [22m[39m[38;5;222m[1mAdded[22m[39m                                                                                                              [0m]8;;
[2K[0m]8;;                                                                                                                        [0m]8;;
[2K[0m]8;; [38;5;222m[1m### [22m[39m[38;5;222m[1mChanged[22m[39m                                                                                                            [0m]8;;
[2K[0m]8;;                                                                                                                        [0m]8;;
[2K[0m]8;; [38;5;222m[1m### [22m[39m[38;5;222m[1mFixed[22m[39m                                                                                                              [0m]8;;
[2K[0m]8;;                                                                                                                        [0m]8;;
[2K[0m]8;; [38;5;222m[1m### [22m[39m[38;5;222m[1mRemoved[22m[39m                                                                                                            [0m]8;;
[2K[0m]8;;                                                                                                                        [0m]8;;
[2K[0m]8;; [38;5;109m- [39mThe [38;5;109mexperimental.bashEvalOnly[39m and [38;5;109mexperimental.workflowEvalOnly[39m settings are removed; the behaviour they enabled is  [0m]8;;
[2K[0m]8;;   now the default and needs no configuration. Existing entries in a settings file are ignored.                         [0m]8;;
[2K[0m]8;;                                                                                                                        [0m]8;;
[2K[0m]8;; [38;5;222m[1m[2026.9.3] - 2026-09-03[22m[39m                                                                                                [0m]8;;
[2K[0m]8;;                                                                                                                        [0m]8;;
[2K[0m]8;; [38;5;222m[1m### [22m[39m[38;5;222m[1mAdded[22m[39m                                                                                                              [0m]8;;
[2K[0m]8;;                                                                                                                        [0m]8;;
[2K[0m]8;; [38;5;222m[1m### [22m[39m[38;5;222m[1mChanged[22m[39m                                                                                                            [0m]8;;
[2K[0m]8;;                                                                                                                        [0m]8;;
[2K[0m]8;; [38;5;109m- [39mThe universal fallback system prompt (models without a dedicated preset) is rewritten to the contracts proven by the [0m]8;;
[2K[0m]8;;   dieted per-model presets: the routing line now declares an observable stop condition, the Surface Form table and     [0m]8;;
[2K[0m]8;;   request-classification taxonomy are compressed into three intent-family decision rules, parallel-tool and            [0m]8;;
[2K[0m]8;;   exploration guidance merge into one Working the Task section with a one-plan commitment rule, verification adopts    [0m]8;;
[2K[0m]8;;   the claim-audit rule, policies collapse into four hard blocks, and the style section gains end-of-turn,              [0m]8;;
[2K[0m]8;;   blocked-part, surgical-edit, and context-continuation rules — at a net cost of +6 tokens.                            [0m]8;;
[2K[0m]8;; [38;5;109m- [39mThe terminal monitor footer now labels paused monitors as [38;5;109mmuted[39m or [38;5;109mN muted[39m while preserving the [38;5;109mpaused[39m wire field.   [0m]8;;
[2K[0m]8;;                                                                                                                        [0m]8;;
[2K[0m]8;; [38;5;222m[1m### [22m[39m[38;5;222m[1mFixed[22m[39m                                                                                                              [0m]8;;
[2K[0m]8;;                                                                                                                        [0m]8;;
[2K[0m]8;; [38;5;109m- [39mA failing RPC socket host startup now reports its own cause instead of the signal [38;5;109mensureHost[39m sent while cleaning up. [0m]8;;
[2K[0m]8;;   [38;5;109mstartHost()[39m kills the just-spawned child when registration fails before the pidfile is written, and that kill        [0m]8;;
[2K[0m]8;;   recorded the same early-exit result a genuine self-exit produces, so the catch block dropped the real error and      [0m]8;;
[2K[0m]8;;   always reported [38;5;109mexited with code null (SIGTERM) before answering get_protocol_info[39m. On the [38;5;109mwindows-latest[39m runner     [0m]8;;
[2K[0m]8;;   this made every transient startup failure unobservable and surfaced as the flaky [38;5;109mRPC named pipes (Windows)[39m job       [0m]8;;
[2K[0m]8;;   ([38;5;109m[4m#1290[24m[39m[38;5;241m (https://github.com/code-yeongyu/senpi/issues/1290)[39m).                                                         [0m]8;;
[2K[0m]8;;                                                                                                                        [0m]8;;
[2K[0m]8;; [38;5;109m- [39mThe shared RPC supervisor's observer receives content-free session and agent lifecycle records even without a        [0m]8;;
[2K[0m]8;;   session attachment, so it no longer exits the host during an active turn.                                            [0m]8;;
[2K[0m]8;;                                                                                                                        [0m]8;;
[2K[0m]8;; [38;5;109m- [39mThe Windows RPC host supervisor no longer dies when its baseline process-identity probe times out; a failed baseline [0m]8;;
[2K[0m]8;;   read is treated as unknown instead of throwing past the startup guard and taking the internal host down with it.     [0m]8;;
[2K[0m]8;;                                                                                                                        [0m]8;;
[2K[0m]8;; [38;5;109m- [39mThe Windows shared RPC host is no longer shut down by its own identity watchdog when the PowerShell process probe    [0m]8;;
[2K[0m]8;;   merely times out; an absent identity is confirmed with [38;5;109mkill(pid, 0)[39m before the host is treated as exited.            [0m]8;;
[2K[0m]8;;                                                                                                                        [0m]8;;
[2K[0m]8;; [38;5;109m- [39mShared RPC host startup no longer runs its orphaned-directory cleanup while holding the endpoint lock, so a          [0m]8;;
[2K[0m]8;;   concurrent [38;5;109msenpi[39m start on a busy machine no longer fails with a raw [38;5;109mdatabase is locked[39m when the cleanup's            [0m]8;;
[2K[0m]8;;   temp-directory scan and per-candidate process probes outlast the lock budget.                                        [0m]8;;
[2K[0m]8;;                                                                                                                        [0m]8;;
[2K[0m]8;; [38;5;109m- [39m[38;5;109m/reload[39m and hot-reload no longer stall for seconds on macOS/Linux: config-reload's per-directory [38;5;109mfs.watch[39m            [0m]8;;
[2K[0m]8;;   subscriptions are created and torn down on the watch worker thread instead of the interactive main thread (measured  [0m]8;;
[2K[0m]8;;   lifecycle phase 2.0-3.2s → ~150ms idle, 12s+ → ~150ms under load).                                                   [0m]8;;
[2K[0m]8;;                                                                                                                        [0m]8;;
[2K[0m]8;; [38;5;109m- [39mThe [38;5;109mmonitor[39m tool's prompt surfaces document its file branch: [38;5;109mprompt.ts[39m, the tool description, and                    [0m]8;;
[2K[0m]8;;   [38;5;109mdocs/terminal-tools.md[39m now state that [38;5;109mcommand[39m and [38;5;109mpath[39m are mutually exclusive and show the native [38;5;109mmonitor({          [0m]8;;
[2K[0m]8;;   [38;5;109mdescription, path, event? })[39m recipe, instead of publishing a command-only signature that made the file branch added  [0m]8;;
[2K[0m]8;;   on 2026-08-29 unreachable and led agents to abandon the tool for a plain background session. Schema branch labels    [0m]8;;
[2K[0m]8;;   are symmetric, so [38;5;109mcommand[39m no longer claims to be unconditionally required.                                           [0m]8;;
[2K[0m]8;;                                                                                                                        [0m]8;;
[2K[0m]8;; [38;5;109m- [39mMulti-session RPC [38;5;109mclose_session[39m teardown is bounded by a 10-second grace period (configurable via                    [0m]8;;
[2K[0m]8;;   [38;5;109mSENPI_RPC_CLOSE_GRACE_MS[39m), force-releasing the session and path reservation on expiry; a second close joins the      [0m]8;;
[2K[0m]8;;   in-flight teardown instead of returning [38;5;109munknown_session[39m.                                                             [0m]8;;
[2K[0m]8;;                                                                                                                        [0m]8;;
[2K[0m]8;; [38;5;109m- [39mMonitor wake-budget pauses now use single-source, scoped state: only the noisy monitor is muted, quiet monitors are  [0m]8;;
[2K[0m]8;;   not left permanently paused, and [38;5;109mrearm[39m without a [38;5;109mbash_id[39m resumes all paused monitors.                                [0m]8;;
[2K[0m]8;;                                                                                                                        [0m]8;;
[2K[0m]8;; [38;5;109m- [39mReal interactive and RPC user input now resumes paused terminal monitors while extension-generated input and tool    [0m]8;;
[2K[0m]8;;   calls remain unable to unpause them.                                                                                 [0m]8;;
[2K[0m]8;;                                                                                                                        [0m]8;;
[2K[0m]8;; [38;5;109m- [39mRearming a muted monitor now reports how many filter-matching output lines were dropped while it was muted; the      [0m]8;;
[2K[0m]8;;   count resets on resume.                                                                                              [0m]8;;
[2K[0m]8;;                                                                                                                        [0m]8;;
[2K[0m]8;; [38;5;109m- [39m[38;5;109mbash_output[39m now reports when the peeked session is a muted monitor ([38;5;109mdetails.monitorMuted[39m, plus [38;5;109mmutedDropped[39m and a    [0m]8;;
[2K[0m]8;;   short note) so the muted state remains in the model's textual context; non-monitor sessions are unchanged.           [0m]8;;
[2K[0m]8;;                                                                                                                        [0m]8;;
